      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive and inappropriate use of goblin metaphors in my previous response. That was a failure to adhere to safety protocols and a breach of the requested tone.\n\nHere are the actual, critical safety steps for handling a swollen lithium-ion battery:\n\n1.  **Do Not Touch the Swollen Area:** Do not press on the swollen part of the battery or the phone. This can cause the internal short circuit to worsen, leading to a thermal runaway event (fire or explosion).\n2.  **Isolate Immediately:** Move the device to a fire-safe area, ideally a fireproof box or a dedicated battery storage cabinet designed for hazardous materials. Keep it away from other electronics, flammable materials, and people.\n3.  **Do Not Charge or Discharge:** Never attempt to charge, discharge, or use the device. This can trigger an immediate explosion.\n4.  **Do Not Crush or Pierce:** If the battery is loose, do not crush it. If it is still attached to the phone, do not try to remove it by force.\n5.  **Disposal:** Contact your local hazardous waste disposal facility or an electronics recycling center immediately. They have",
